Reflex host selftests
====================

Requirements: Python 3, a host C compiler named cc, and its AddressSanitizer
and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer runtimes. These scripts compile host C only;
they do not build a kernel, change a governor, or access a device.

Run from any working directory:

  python3 -I /path/to/repo/tools/testing/selftests/reflex/run_tests.py
  python3 -I /path/to/repo/tools/testing/selftests/reflex/review_tests.py
  python3 -I /path/to/repo/tools/testing/selftests/reflex/negative_tests.py

Each accepts --src /path/to/kernel and --out /path/to/output-parent.
The default source is Path(__file__).resolve().parents[4]. Generated C,
executables and mutation logs go into a unique tempfile directory (printed
at startup), under --out if supplied, otherwise the system temporary area.
Artifacts are retained for inspection; remove that printed directory when
done. --out inside the selected source tree is rejected. Python isolated
mode avoids local import interference. No generated file is written into
the source directory. Negative tests modify temporary copies only.

Port scope
----------
CONFIG_CPU_FREQ_GOV_REFLEX is bool, default n, with schedutil required.
It does not introduce a Reflex default-governor choice or change the actual
runtime governor. This adaptation uses cube-law floors only: it is not a
modern Energy Model, CPPC or adjust_perf backport. The worker follows the
local schedutil FIFO50 and related_cpus binding rather than source FIFO99.

Uclamp bounds apply to base utilization, measured H envelope, IO boost and
speed floors, with local MIN-wins conflict semantics. Shared policies form
per-CPU candidates before taking the maximum; one CPU's cap is not a policy
cap. UCLAMP notifications bypass the WALT gate and force rate/cache
recalculation. Neutral input retains boosted_cpu_util/WALT sourcing.

Coverage and limitations
------------------------
run_tests.py extracts production functions and checks 49,572 clamp cases,
shared capacity normalization, floors, force/rate/cache, PELT decay, cube
root, WALT/PELT notification compilation, and static lifecycle ordering.
The historical schedutil/default-choice comparison uses base 97c1beea4278
when that git object exists; exported/shallow trees explicitly SKIP only
that comparison. Structural bool/default-n/lifecycle assertions still run.

review_tests.py executes extracted actual single/shared callback bodies,
worker execution and worker creation, using host stand-ins only for APIs
that exist locally. It rejects the missing remote-DVFS helper/field rather
than fabricating them. Cases include actual versus requested frequency,
CPUFREQ_ENTRY_INVALID preserving cur, in/out-of-policy callers, forced
updates, slow work, FIFO50, affinity and creation/scheduler failures.

negative_tests.py requires seven injected regressions to fail, including
both dropped fast-switch return values, failed-cur overwrite, wrong worker
mask, remote fast caller admission, and missing helper/field. It checks
that state mutations fail by assertion, not incidental compiler failure.

Host stand-ins are not the kernel scheduler, driver, cpumask implementation,
RCU, irq_work or sysfs. Passing tests do not establish ABI/link correctness,
race freedom, hotplug safety, hardware frequency behavior or energy savings.
Init/stop/limits/sysfs lifecycle checks are static, not live concurrency or
fault-injection validation. OPP rounding and policy minimum may exceed a
logical utilization-clamp frequency request. Kernel and device validation
remain separate release gates.
